comment_body:
@anders-nexthop thanks for the second round. All 11 inline threads are fixed in b182fd278, replied to and resolved. On the review body's two headline items:

- **A manually removed `skip-wf-issue-expired` never comes back.** Fixed. An issue the workflow has already expired gets the label back on the next run, without a second expiry comment.
- **The runner force-checks-out the caller's working tree.** Fixed. Branches are now read from git objects (`ls-tree`/`cat-file` on `origin/<branch>`), so neither the expiry runner nor the close guard touches HEAD or the working tree.

@opcoder0, on your label-history question: rather than have the stale workflow read label history, the expiry manager now treats the label as state it maintains. If someone removes it from a tracked issue that has expired, the next daily run puts it back. So the stale bot's label exemption stays accurate, apart from the window until that run.

Housekeeping: I rewrote the branch to drop a `Co-Authored-By` trailer that was failing EasyCLA. The content is unchanged. The commits referenced in round 1 are now 61f41f751 (was f209b1860) and ba7f9c740 (was 905dc498a).

@bingwang-ms About the two CodeQL "wrong number of arguments" alerts: `sai_qos_tests.py` imports from `switch`, and CodeQL resolved these calls against the legacy Python 2 `tests/saitests/switch.py`, where `sai_thrift_read_port_counters(client, port)` takes 2 arguments. PTF runs these tests with `--test-dir saitests/py3`, so the module actually used is `tests/saitests/py3/switch.py`, where the signature is `sai_thrift_read_port_counters(client, asic_type, port)`, and the calls were correct at runtime.

The flagged calls were inside a nested helper function in `QSharedWatermarkTest.runTest`; the other calls in the file are made directly in the test methods and are not flagged. e993c11b9 moves the two reads inline into `runTest`, with no change to the computed values or assertions, so the CodeQL check should pass now.

The same change is applied to the msft-202512 manual cherry-pick, Azure/sonic-mgmt.msft#1507.

@lolyu @nhegde-microsoft how do we want to proceed here?

Adding VPP to acl_l2 skips the RX_DRP check in `test_drop_counters.py::test_acl_drop / test_acl_egress_drop`, so the (fixed) tests now pass. If that's the direction we want to go (and accept that ACL drops land in RX_DRP on VPP), we just need to update `test_upstream_standby_rx_drop_check` to likewise skip the check for any acl_l2 platforms.

If not, we would go with https://github.com/sonic-net/sonic-platform-vpp/pull/280 and have the standby report no RX_DRP. I would use this current PR to simply fix the `test_drop_counters.py` test in this case. Let me know how you'd like to proceed.

